<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Military Government of Santo Domingo</h1><p class="st-meta">1916 CE – 1924 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The US occupation of the Dominican Republic from 1916 to 1924, which modernised roads and built the National Guard from which Rafael Trujillo rose.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After years of civil war and default on foreign debt, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">Marines</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in May 1916. When the Dominican congress refused American conditions, Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-shepard-knapp">Harry Shepard Knapp</a> proclaimed a military government in November 1916, ending the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-dominican-republic">Second Dominican Republic</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>US naval officers ruled by decree, with Dominican ministers replaced by Americans. They disarmed the population, censored the press, built highways linking the main towns, expanded schools and reorganised finances. A land law favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> companies, many of them American. In the eastern provinces peasant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrillas</a> called gavilleros resisted until 1922 and were met with harsh repression. A new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-guard-dominican-republic">National Guard</a> was trained, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-trujillo">Rafael Trujillo</a> rose quickly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hughes-peynado-plan">Hughes-Peynado plan</a> of 1922, a provisional Dominican government was installed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horacio-vasquez">Horacio Vásquez</a> won elections in 1924, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-marine-corps">Marines</a> left in September 1924, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/third-dominican-republic">Third Dominican Republic</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/military-government-of-santo-domingo.jpg" alt="Military Government of Santo Domingo" width="320" height="169"><figcaption>Image: Created by jacobolus using Adobe Illustrator. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_United_States_(1912-1959).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1916 CE – 1924 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Harry Shepard Knapp</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>United States occupation of the Dominican Republic</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=military-government-of-santo-domingo" aria-label="Military Government of Santo Domingo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Military Government of Santo Domingo lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749M3105 1749L2570 1008M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0M2570 1008h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1920&amp;focus=military-government-of-santo-domingo">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
